            <ArticleTitle>Effects of myofascial-meridian stimulation therapy (mmst) on shoulder pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1175-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Fifty patients with shoulder pain were classified into two groups according to the type of pain: a capsular-pattern group (N = 22) and a non-capsular-pattern group (N = 28). Both groups received myofascial-meridian stimulation therapy four times in two weeks. After therapy, the pain level was significantly lower in the capsular-pattern than in the non-capsular-pattern group. The capsular-pattern group showed a nonsignificant trend toward greater range of motion than the non-capsular-pattern group. These data suggest that myofascial-meridian stimulation therapy might be useful as a new treatment to reduce pain and increase range of motion in patients with shoulder pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
